  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nCurrently, low cloud remains across central and eastern North\nDakota, with only some high clouds in the west. Temperatures\nwere mainly in the 60s , except low to mid 70s under the more\nabundant sunshine out west. \n\nFor tonight, most guidance suggests that the stratus will work\nback to the west as a south to southeast return flow strengthens\nover North Dakota ahead of a surface low situated over western \nCanada. We do not anticipated widespread dense fog like we have \nseen the past two mornings because of the increasing surface \ngradient. However, some patchy fog can not be ruled out. \nGuidance has been suggesting southern portions of the forecast \narea, and added a mention of patchy fog later tonight into \nSaturday morning. Latest guidance also keeps the mention of rain\nfrom the Turtle Mountains into the James River Valley and \neastward late this afternoon through tonight. There is a \ntimeframe later this evening where low pops do extend back to \nnear the Missouri River in the south central, before shifting \nback to the east after midnight. With the saturated lower layers\nand some weak low level omega, we could see some patchy drizzle\nextending west of the main precipitation area, to the Highway \n83 corridor. Will monitor, but too uncertain to add a mention at\nthis time. On Saturday, the cold front associated with the \nCanadian low pressure system swings west to east through the \nforecast area. This will clear out the low stratus and shift \nwinds from southerly to northwest. Although it won't be much \ncolder behind the front, it will be noticeably drier. Highs will\nbe mainly in the upper 60s to mid 70s across the forecast area \nSaturday. Look for breezy southerly winds central early in the \nday and breezy northwest winds over western ND late morning and \ninto the afternoon. High pressure builds over the area Saturday \nnight through a good portion of the day Sunday. Look for lows in\nthe lower 40s to lower 50s Saturday night and highs on Sunday \nin the mid 60s to lower 70s.\n\nAnother upper level trough then looks to impact our weather\nlater in the day Sunday, through Monday night. A lead shortwave\npropagating through a northern California upper trough will\ninduce cyclogenesis over the central High Plains, with an\ninverted surface trough extending into the northern Plains by\nearly Monday. A northern stream wave may induce convection over\nthe Mondak region late Sunday, and into Sunday evening. Strong \nbulk shear will be situated over the area. MLCAPE of \n500-1000J/KG lifts from South Dakota into southwest ND Sunday \nafternoon. Currently we have a general thunderstorm risk form \nSPC Sunday. A stronger storm can not be ruled out with some hail\nthe most likely threat due to the likely elevated nature of the\nstorms. The entire system spreads east Sunday night through \nMonday. Monday afternoon into early Monday may be another \npotential for stronger storms as strong bulk shear weakens a \nlittle but instability maybe greater from the south central into\nthe James River Valley. As mentioned in the previous \ndiscussion, anomalously high Precipitable Water values and\npossibly some training storms, may result in efficient rainers \nwith locally heavy rainfall Sunday night through Monday night as\nthe system works it's way east across the forecast area.\n\nWe then dry out, at least for a while with a quasi-zonal flow\nover the region and temperatures remaining near normal with\nhighs in the 60s to lower 70s and lows in the 40s through the\nmiddle portion of the workweek.",
